# Ratio - 128 patches, six operators and thirty-two ways of wiring them.
#
# Each operator has sixteen waveforms, eight modes, a ratio from a quarter to
# sixty-four, its own feedback and its own four-stage envelope. On top of that
# are two algorithms at once with a morph between them, and snap and skew on
# the ratios.
#
# Families in tab order: keys (20), bass (18), lead (18), pluck (14), bell
# (18), reed (14), pad (16) and texture (10). Within a family the patches
# run plainest to strangest, and the strange ones are what this machine
# has of its own: the morph between two algorithms, the skew that fans the
# partials apart, `snap` set to none, and the ring, fold, sync, filter,
# phase and crush modes.
#
# **Bells get their own demo.** A struck sound is its tail, and every other
# phrase here starts the next note before the last one has finished. `bell`
# leaves a second and a half between strikes, puts two together so two tails
# beat, and leaves the last one ringing on its own.
#
# Levelled to the house figure on the loudness column, the same as every
# other machine in the app. Part of that came out of the machine's own output
# (`kHouse` in Ratio.cpp) so Init lands there too. A few patches sit one to
# six decibels under and they are either a single operator with nothing
# modulating it or a strike short enough that four hundred milliseconds of it
# is mostly silence.
#
# A ratio is a multiplier on the note, so a carrier's is almost always one and
# everything interesting happens in the operators feeding it. A carrier at
# half or four times plays the wrong octave, and a fixed carrier plays the
# same pitch whatever key is held. The range sweep is what finds these.
#
# Every patch but Init has eight named macros. Where a macro at full moved
# the level by more than a few decibels, an amp route on it takes that
# back out. A morph macro needs `algob` set to where it goes; at morph 0
# the second algorithm has no effect. Lines under "Silent until a macro
# reaches them" set up an operator at level 0 for a macro to bring in.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Ratio` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Ratio
role    keys

patch Init family=keys

patch "Soft Keys" family=keys  note=60 range=36..84 role=keys
  set macro1 "Bright"
  set macro2 "Dark"
  set macro3 "Octave"
  set macro4 "Twelfth"
  set macro5 "Growl"
  set macro6 "Morph"
  set macro7 "Skew"
  set macro8 "Metal"
  # Macros: each knob adds its depth to the destinations below.
  mac1_d1  #17
  mac1_a1  0.35
  mac2_d1  #17
  mac2_a1  -0.25
  mac3_d1  #5
  mac3_a1  0.3
  mac4_d1  #6
  mac4_a1  0.35
  mac5_d1  #16
  mac5_a1  0.3
  mac6_d1  #2
  mac6_a1  0.4
  mac7_d1  #3
  mac7_a1  0.12
  mac8_d1  #12
  mac8_a1  0.12
  # Almost no modulation at all: two carriers a fifth apart, which is a
  # machine like this being asked to be simple. It is the patch the rest
  # of the bank is measured against.
  algoa          #22
  o1_ratio       1
  o1_level       1.00
  o1_decay       2.5 s
  o1_sustain     0.35
  o2_ratio       2
  o2_level       0.59
  o2_decay       2 s
  o2_sustain     0.25
  o3_ratio       3
  o3_level       0.23
  o3_decay       1.5 s
  o3_sustain     0.1
  o4_level       0.00
  o5_level       0.00
  o6_level       0.00
  f_type         #2
  f_freq         3000 Hz
  volume         1.000
  m01_src        #1
  m01_dest       #19
  m01_depth      0.175
  # Morph goes to one chain, so the partials modulate each other.
  algob          #0

patch "Mellow Keys" family=keys  note=60 range=36..84 role=keys
  set macro1 "Bright"
  set macro2 "Bell"
  set macro3 "Soft"
  set macro4 "Dark"
  set macro5 "Growl"
  set macro6 "Skew"
  set macro7 "Morph"
  set macro8 "Metal"
